		<div class="texto-blog">					
		<p>In this article we will see <strong>How fixtures work in the symfony 5 framework</strong>, to work with test data in our databases.</p>

		<h2>How to create fixtures in symfony 5?</h2>

		{{ include('inc/ad_defect.html.twig') }} 	
		
		<p>First we have to install the package with <strong>composer</strong>, we execute the command in a terminal, in the root of the project.</p>

		<pre class="text-code">
		<code>
	composer require --dev doctrine/doctrine-fixtures-bundle
		</code>
		</pre>

		<p>Then we will create the file for the fixtures.</p>

		<pre class="text-code">
		<code>
	php bin/console make:fixtures
		</code>
		</pre>

		<p>We put a name to the fixtures, for example <strong>UserFixture</strong>.</p>

		<p>Now we will create the basic structure in the following location <code>nameProject/src/DataFixtures/UserFixtures.php</code>, we will create a class and a method, I have added the values with which we are going to work inside the class.</p>
	
		<pre class="text-code">
		<code class="">
	namespace App\DataFixtures;

	// we use the user entity, first we need to create it
	use App\Entity\User;

	use Doctrine\Bundle\FixturesBundle\Fixture;
	use Doctrine\Persistence\ObjectManager;

	class UserFixtures extends Fixture
	{

		public function load(ObjectManager $manager)
		{  
			// we create an instance
			$user = new User();

			// we set 3 properties from the entity
			$user->setFirstName('jonathan');
			$user->setLastName('castro');
			$user->setEmail('admin@hotmail.com');		

			$manager->persist($user);		

			$manager->flush();
		}
	}
		</code>
		</pre>

		<h2>How to load the fixtures?</h2>		

		<p>Finally we load the fixtures with the test data.</p>


		<pre class="text-code">
		<code>
	php bin/console doctrine:fixtures:load 
		</code>
		</pre>

